---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Brand from "../components/Brand.astro";
import BookingLink from "../components/BookingLink.astro";
const colors = [
  {
    name: "White",
    hex: "#FFFFFF",
    role: "Page and section backgrounds",
    className: "swatch-white",
  },
  {
    name: "Logo orange",
    hex: "#E77539",
    role: "Buttons, closing CTA, and decorative accents",
    className: "swatch-orange",
  },
  {
    name: "Large-type logo orange",
    hex: "#E67438",
    role: "Large headings only · 3.03:1 on white",
    className: "swatch-action",
  },
  {
    name: "Logo blue",
    hex: "#243A6C",
    role: "Hero focal point, labels, and focus rings",
    className: "swatch-blue",
  },
  {
    name: "Text gray",
    hex: "#292929",
    role: "Headings and body text",
    className: "swatch-ink",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Design System & Phase 1 Review | Truva Solutions"
  description="Local design review: Truva typography, sampled logo colors, components, assumptions, and the next phases."
>
  <main id="main" class="shell review-page">
    <Brand />
    <p class="review-status">LOCAL PREVIEW · PHASE 1 · NOT FOR PUBLICATION</p>
    <h1>A quieter kind of confidence.</h1>
    <p class="review-intro">
      A minimal connected diagram places your product and processes at the
      center, with thin orange lines to AI security, cloud security, and
      compliance. Small blue labels and line icons sit on white.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/">
      ← Back to the homepage
    </a>
    <section>
      <h2>Colors from the real logo.</h2>
      <p>
        The supplied PNG has gradients, not flat brand colors. The three
        chromatic colors below are exact opaque pixels sampled from{" "}
        <code>truva-logo-fixed.png</code>. They are representative selections,
        not a claim that a formal brand palette exists.
      </p>
      <div class="swatches">
        {colors.map((color) => (
          <div>
            <div class:list={["swatch-color", color.className]}></div>
            <strong>{color.name}</strong>
            <p>{color.hex}</p>
            <p class="placeholder-note">{color.role}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="review-intro">
        Secondary text: #626262. Borders: #E1E1E1. The bright logo orange is
        just under 3:1 on white. Buttons and the orange CTA band use dark text.
        Large orange headings use the adjacent logo sample #E67438 (3.03:1).
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Arial. Nothing extra to download.</h2>
      <p>
        Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif. Regular (400) and bold (700) only. Body
        line-height 1.6; text width up to 65ch. Hierarchy comes from scale,
        spacing, and weight.
      </p>
      <BookingLink />
      <p class="review-intro">
        Buttons have visible keyboard focus. The mobile navigation works without
        JavaScript; a small script adds close-on-selection and Escape.
        Reduced-motion preferences disable smooth scrolling.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Decisions for this first preview.</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Astro static + Tailwind.</strong> A separate marketing folder
          preserves the current site and Python backend.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Positioning:</strong> startup security, AI, cloud, and
          compliance. “25+ years” and senior delivery come from your brief; the
          author bio and supporting credentials remain an input.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Services only:</strong> Products and the proposed Compass
          concept have been removed. Home returns to the homepage; Services
          opens a dropdown.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Booking:</strong> email requests to the existing public
          business address until you provide a Calendly or Cal.com account URL.
          No appointment is booked automatically.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Proof:</strong> TODO blocks instead of invented client logos,
          results, or testimonials. Remove unfilled proof sections before
          launch.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Process:</strong> “Prepare for audit” replaces “Certify” so
          the site does not imply Truva issues SOC 2 reports or ISO
          certifications.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Existing articles:</strong> cards link to their current public
          URLs. No blog URL or production content has changed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No personal details:</strong> business email and US service
          area only.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Review now. Build the rest next.</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Phase 1 — ready for review:</strong> homepage, visual system,
          responsive navigation, metadata, logo derivatives, and OG card.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Phase 2 — after review:</strong> five service pages and
          evidence-based keyword research. Volume, difficulty, and existing
          rankings are not yet verified.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Phase 3:</strong> approved products, clients, about, blog
          preservation, resources, and author profiles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Phase 4:</strong> real booking and forms, server-validated
          Turnstile, analytics, redirects, sitemap, full schema, security
          headers, and launch verification.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="review-intro">
        The preview is noindex. Local scores do not establish production Core
        Web Vitals or search rankings. Cloudflare migration and backend routing
        require a separate release review.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
